            "title": "China’s economy off to a bad start to 2019, analysts say",
            "snippets": [
              "The monthly purchasing managers’ index (PMI) is among the few indicators available to check the health of the Chinese economy at the beginning of each year.\nThe monthly purchasing managers’ index (PMI) is among the few indicators available to check the health of the Chinese economy at the beginning of each year.",
              "# China economy ‘losing momentum’ as first major data of 2019 paints gloomy picture\n- Chinese manufacturing contracted in January, for the second month in a row, purchasing managers’ index (PMI) data showed\n- The downward pressure on the Chinese economy is still significant, according to Li Chao, an analyst with Huatai Securities\n\nChina economy\n\n+ FOLLOW\n\nOrange Wang Sidney Leng\n\nOrange Wang and Sidney Leng\n\nPublished: 1:01pm, 31 Jan, 2019",
              "Updated: 10:47pm, 31 Jan, 2019\n\nWhy you can trust SCMP\n\nLink Copied\n\nPost\n\nThe monthly purchasing managers’ index (PMI) is among the few indicators available to check the health of the Chinese economy at the beginning of each year.",
              "The monthly purchasing managers’ index (PMI) is among the few indicators available to check the health of the Chinese economy at the beginning of each year.",
              "{\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"South China Morning Post\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"genre\":\"Economy\",\"headline\":\"China economy ‘losing momentum’ as first major data of 2019 paints gloomy picture\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"Orange Wang\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.scmp.com/author/orange-wang\"},\"locationCreated\":{\"name\":\"Hong Kong\"},\"dateCreated\":\"2019-01-31T13:01:40+08:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-01-31T22:47:48+08:00\",\"datePublished\":\"2019-01-31T13:01:40+08:00\",\"alternativeHeadline\":\"China’s economy off to a bad start to 2019, analysts say\",\"description\":\"China’s first major economic announcement of 2019 showed a slight rebound in manufacturing and a stronger-than-expected services sector, but analysts said there were clear signs of pressure on the Chinese economy in January.\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\",\"articleBody\":\"The National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) does not release other economic data for January and February, such as industrial added value, retail sales, and fixed asset investment, until early-March, to avoid any distortion due to the Lunar New Year holiday. The PMI saw a slight improvement in January, from 49.4 in December to 49.5, but this could not keep the manufacturing sector for contracting for a second successive time with a reading below 50 indicating shrinkage. There is some room for optimism in the over-performance of the service sector, with non-manufacturing PMI growing from 53.8 to 54.7. But analysts have, on the whole, greeted the data negatively. Li Chao, an analyst with brokerage house Huatai Securities said that the indicators were gloomy, and expected the picture to remain so for the foreseeable future. “That means the downward pressure on the Chinese economy is still significant,” he wrote in a note. China’s manufacturing shrank again in January, as fears of further economic slowdown mount “Companies are continuing to cut their stock proactively, a move that might not change until the fourth quarter of 2019, meaning weaker support for the gross domestic product,” Li added, commenting on the rebound in stock levels found in the manufacturing index. Marcel Thieliant, senior economist at Capital Economics, warned that the data may be inaccurate, saying that “we are wary of putting too much weight on the official PMIs given that they have provided false signals in the past”. Another metric, the Caixin manufacturing PMI, will be released on Friday and should help provide a fuller picture. “While the official manufacturing PMI did not weaken any further in January, it still suggests that the economy lost momentum at the start of the year,” Thieliant said. Economists at the Japanese investment bank Nomura suggested that the weak export sales figures among the PMI data point to the end of front-loading. Front-loading was a strategy used by many companies as a way of avoiding the US tariffs on Chinese goods. It involves shipping cargoes earlier than they are required, so that they arrived in the US before the tariffs were enforceable. The first raft of US tariffs were implemented in July. This means the Chinese economy will slow further still over the course of 2019, Nomura’s analysts said. “[The economy is] dragged by synchronised shocks of the ongoing credit downcycle, the end of the front-loaded exports, the cooling property sector, and the end of the replacement cycle of consumer durables,” they wrote. The picture is markedly worse for small and medium sized companies, which experienced a rougher start to 2019 than China’s larger companies, official data shows. The growth in non-manufacturing PMI, meanwhile, has been attributed to seasonal issues, with Chinese shoppers spending more money in the run up to the holiday period “Boosts from consumer spending during the Lunar New Year holiday just emerged,” said Zhao Heqing, senior statistician of the NBS. The holiday effect and so-called “consumption upgrade” have boosted telecoms, banks, tourism, postal services, and transport, Zhao said. He added that these will wear off, as cold weather and the return of migrant workers to rural areas for the Lunar New Year starts to hit consumption.\",\"articleSection\":\"China Economy\",\"hasPart\":{\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\"}}"
            ]
